    {
      "title": "Requirement 4: named loggers from one registry",
      "text": "The factory hands out one logger per name using `computeIfAbsent`, so concurrent first use still yields a single instance. Loggers share the factory's appender list, so an appender added later reaches loggers created earlier. `getInstance()` is the shared default; the public constructor exists for tests and dependency injection.",
      "code": [
        {
          "file": "LoggerFactory.java",
          "body": "import java.time.Clock;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;\n\n/** One registry of named loggers. getInstance() is the shared default; the constructor exists for tests and DI. */\npublic class LoggerFactory {\n    private static class Holder {\n        static final LoggerFactory INSTANCE = new LoggerFactory(LogLevel.INFO, Clock.systemUTC());\n    }\n\n    public static LoggerFactory getInstance() {\n        return Holder.INSTANCE;\n    }\n\n    private final Map<String, Logger> loggers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    private final List<Appender> appenders = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n    private final Clock clock;\n    private volatile LogLevel defaultLevel;\n\n    public LoggerFactory(LogLevel defaultLevel, Clock clock) {\n        this.defaultLevel = defaultLevel;\n        this.clock = clock;\n    }\n\n    public void setDefaultLevel(LogLevel level) { this.defaultLevel = level; }\n\n    public void addAppender(Appender appender) {\n        appenders.add(appender);\n    }\n\n    /** The same name always returns the same logger, even under concurrent first use. */\n    public Logger getLogger(String name) {\n        return loggers.computeIfAbsent(name, n -> new Logger(n, defaultLevel, appenders, clock));\n    }\n\n    public void shutdown() {\n        for (Appender a : appenders) {\n            a.close();\n        }\n    }\n}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Concurrency and extensibility: decorators",
      "text": "Both are `Appender` decorators, so no existing class changes. `LevelFilterAppender` forwards only events at or above its own threshold, e.g. ERROR to an alert file. `AsyncAppender` puts a bounded queue and a worker thread in front of a slow destination: application threads only ever `offer`, and when the queue is full the event is dropped and counted instead of blocking the caller. `close()` drains the queue before stopping.",
      "code": [
        {
          "file": "LevelFilterAppender.java",
          "body": "/** Decorator: only forwards events at or above its own threshold, e.g. ERROR+ to an alert file. */\npublic class LevelFilterAppender implements Appender {\n    private final LogLevel min;\n    private final Appender delegate;\n\n    public LevelFilterAppender(LogLevel min, Appender delegate) {\n        this.min = min;\n        this.delegate = delegate;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void append(LogEvent event) {\n        if (event.level().isAtLeast(min)) {\n            delegate.append(event);\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void close() {\n        delegate.close();\n    }\n}"
        },
        {
          "file": "AsyncAppender.java",
          "body": "import java.time.Instant;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;\n\n/**\n * Decorator that moves slow I/O off the caller's thread. Application threads only ever\n * offer to a bounded queue; when it is full the event is dropped and counted, never waited on.\n */\npublic class AsyncAppender implements Appender {\n    private static final LogEvent STOP = new LogEvent(Instant.EPOCH, LogLevel.DEBUG, \"\", \"\", \"\", null);\n\n    private final Appender delegate;\n    private final BlockingQueue<LogEvent> queue;\n    private final Thread worker;\n    private final AtomicLong dropped = new AtomicLong();\n\n    public AsyncAppender(Appender delegate, int capacity) {\n        this.delegate = delegate;\n        this.queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(capacity);\n        this.worker = new Thread(this::drain, \"log-async-worker\");\n        this.worker.setDaemon(true);\n        this.worker.start();\n    }\n\n    public long dropped() { return dropped.get(); }\n\n    @Override\n    public void append(LogEvent event) {\n        if (!queue.offer(event)) {\n            dropped.incrementAndGet();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void drain() {\n        try {\n            while (true) {\n                LogEvent e = queue.take();\n                if (e == STOP) return;\n                try {\n                    delegate.append(e);\n                } catch (RuntimeException ex) {\n                    // a failing destination must not kill the worker\n                }\n            }\n        } catch (InterruptedException ex) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    /** Flushes everything already queued, then stops the worker and closes the delegate. */\n    @Override\n    public void close() {\n        try {\n            queue.put(STOP);\n            worker.join();\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        }\n        delegate.close();\n    }\n}"
        }
      ]
    }
